/* Deeper explanations for each kind of part, keyed by the part's name.
 * Level 1 is the part's own `say` line in its device file. These are levels 2 to 5. */
TAL.learn = {
  'Antenna': [
    'Radio and TV signals are invisible waves that travel through the air. A piece of metal sticks out and catches a tiny bit of them, a bit like a net catching rain.',
    'An antenna is usually a metal rod or a loop of wire. The passing waves nudge the electricity inside the metal back and forth, and that tiny wiggle goes down a cable to the tuner.',
    'A radio wave has an electric field and a magnetic field that oscillate together. When the wave hits a conductor, it pushes electrons back and forth, creating a very weak alternating current at the same frequency as the wave.',
    'Antenna length matters. A common rule is that an antenna works best when it is about half or a quarter of the wavelength it is catching. The signal that arrives is often only microvolts to millivolts, so the tuner amplifies it right away.',
  ],
  'Back cover': [
    'The back cover is like the lid on a box. It keeps fingers away from the parts inside and keeps dust out.',
    'It is usually molded plastic, held on with screws or little snap tabs. Slots near the top let warm air escape so the parts inside do not get too hot.',
  ],
  'Battery label': [
    'The label is a thin wrapper that tells you what kind of battery this is and which end is which.',
    'It is a printed film wrapped around the metal can. It also keeps the can from touching other metal parts by accident, which could make a short circuit.',
  ],
  'Battery': [
    'A battery is a little tank of stored energy. When you switch the flashlight on, the battery pushes electricity through the wires to the light.',
    'Inside, two different materials sit in a paste, with a thin wall between them. When a wire connects the two ends, a chemical reaction starts and pushes electrons through the wire.',
    'A battery turns chemical energy into electrical energy. The reaction moves electrons from the negative end through the outside circuit to the positive end, and that flow is current. A standard AA cell pushes with about 1.5 volts.',
    'A typical alkaline AA cell has a zinc powder anode and a manganese dioxide cathode in an alkaline electrolyte, and stores around 2 to 3 amp hours at low drain. Voltage slowly sags as it empties, and two cells in a row give about 3 volts.',
  ],
  'Big capacitor': [
    'A capacitor is like a tiny rechargeable bucket for electricity. It fills up and then lets go smoothly, which keeps the power steady.',
    'Think of a water tank that smooths out a pump that pushes in pulses. The capacitor soaks up the bumps and gives out an even flow, so the screen and sound do not flicker or hum.',
    'A capacitor stores energy in an electric field between two metal plates separated by an insulator. Bigger plates hold more charge, which is measured in farads. In power parts, a large one smooths the lumpy direct current made from wall power.',
    'Large power supply capacitors are usually aluminum electrolytic, with rolled foil and a liquid electrolyte, often several hundred microfarads and rated for hundreds of volts. Charged to about 170 volts or more, they can hold a shock for a while after unplugging.',
  ],
  'Bridge rectifier': [
    'Wall electricity keeps changing direction, but the parts inside a TV want it to flow one way. This part turns it around so it only goes forward.',
    'It is four one-way gates called diodes, wired in a diamond. Whichever way the wall power is pushing, two of the gates open and send the current down the same path.',
    'Wall power is alternating current, and in North America it goes back and forth 60 times a second, which means it reverses direction 120 times a second. A bridge rectifier flips the negative halves upward, giving pulsing direct current that a capacitor can then smooth.',
    'Each diode drops about 0.7 volts when conducting, so a bridge loses roughly 1.4 volts and a little heat. A 120 volt outlet peaks near 170 volts, so the rectifier must be rated well above that.',
  ],
  'Button cap': [
    'This is the part of the button you actually push with your finger.',
    'It is a small piece of plastic that sits on top of a switch. When you press it, it moves down and squeezes the switch underneath, and a spring pushes it back up.',
  ],
  'Channel knob': [
    'Turning this knob changes which show you see. Each click picks a different channel.',
    'On an old TV the knob is connected to the tuner. As you turn it, it picks a different spot to listen, like turning the dial on a radio.',
  ],
  'Clock crystal': [
    'This tiny piece of crystal acts like a super steady metronome. The chips use its ticking to keep time.',
    'A thin slice of quartz is held in a small metal can. When you give it a little electricity, it vibrates the same number of times every second, and the chips count those wiggles.',
    'Quartz is piezoelectric, which means squeezing it makes a voltage and applying a voltage makes it flex. Cut to a precise shape, it rings at one very exact frequency, and a circuit keeps it ringing as a stable clock signal.',
    'Common crystals run at frequencies like 24 or 25 million cycles per second for processors, or 32,768 cycles per second for keeping time. Typical accuracy is around 20 parts per million, which is better than most other cheap timing parts by a large margin.',
  ],
  'Coil': [
    'A coil is wire wound round and round like a spring. When electricity flows through it, it becomes a little magnet.',
    'Winding wire into loops makes the magnetic effect much stronger, because every loop adds to the others. Engineers use coils to store energy, to filter out noise, and to move energy from one place to another.',
    'A current makes a magnetic field, and a changing magnetic field pushes back on the current. This effect is called inductance and is measured in henries. A coil resists sudden changes in current, so it can smooth or filter signals.',
    'Power coils are often wound on a ferrite core, a ceramic magnetic material that concentrates the field and works well at high frequency. Inductance values range from a few microhenries to millihenries, and designers watch for the core saturating when too much current flows.',
  ],
  'Color chip': [
    'This chip figures out the colors in the picture. It decides how much red, green and blue each dot should have.',
    'The signal arrives with the brightness and the color mixed together. The chip separates them and works out how much red, green and blue to send, so the colors look right.',
    'Color pictures are made by mixing red, green and blue light. The chip decodes the color information carried in the signal, then adjusts brightness, contrast and tint before sending the three color signals on.',
    'In analog broadcast, the color information rides on a subcarrier of about 3.58 megahertz in the American standard. Decoders lock onto a short burst of that subcarrier sent at the start of each line to get the hue exactly right. Modern sets do similar processing digitally.',
  ],
  'Deflection yoke': [
    'The yoke steers the beam of the picture tube. It bends the beam so that it can reach every spot on the screen.',
    'Around the neck of the tube are two sets of coils. They make magnets that push the beam left and right, and up and down, so it can sweep over the whole screen.',
    'The beam is a stream of electrons, which are charged particles. A magnetic field pushes moving charges sideways, so changing the current in the coils changes the field and moves the beam. This is called magnetic deflection.',
    'The horizontal coils are driven by a sawtooth current at about 15,734 times per second in an American color set, so the beam sweeps across each line. The vertical coils run at about 60 times per second. The coils are wound on a ferrite core to guide the magnetic field.',
  ],
  'Degaussing coil': [
    'This big loop of wire helps clean up the colors. When the TV turns on, it gives the tube a quick magnetic shake.',
    'Metal parts inside the tube can slowly pick up stray magnetism, which makes the colors look blotchy. When you switch on, the coil makes a strong, fading magnetic buzz that wipes that magnetism away.',
    'Degaussing means removing unwanted magnetization. An alternating current that starts strong and fades to zero makes a field that flips back and forth, each time a little weaker, and leaves the metal with almost no magnetism.',
    'At power on, the coil gets an alternating current that decays over about a second, often controlled by a thermistor whose resistance rises as it heats. The point is to demagnetize the steel shadow mask so the beams land on the right color dots.',
  ],
  'Diffuser plate': [
    'The diffuser spreads out the light so the screen glows evenly, instead of showing bright spots where the lights are.',
    'It is a thick sheet of clear plastic with tiny bumps or particles inside. They scatter the light in all directions, so a row of small lights looks like one smooth glow.',
    'Scattering happens when light hits tiny particles or rough surfaces and bounces in many directions. The diffuser uses this to turn many point sources into one even sheet of light behind the panel.',
    'Diffusers are often polystyrene or acrylic with embedded scattering particles, often one to a few millimeters thick. A stack of thin films above it, such as prism sheets, then steers the light toward the viewer. Spacing from the LEDs trades evenness against thinness.',
  ],
  'Diode': [
    'A diode is a one-way street for electricity. It lets current go forward but not backward.',
    'Inside is a tiny piece of special material with two layers. Current can cross from one layer to the other in only one direction, much like a door that only opens one way.',
    'A diode is a semiconductor junction between p-type material, which has spare holes, and n-type material, which has spare electrons. Forward voltage pushes charge across the junction, while reverse voltage is blocked.',
    'A silicon diode starts to conduct at about 0.6 to 0.7 volts forward, and a Schottky diode at around 0.3 volts, which wastes less power. In a switching power supply, fast diodes rectify high frequency pulses and have to survive hundreds of volts in reverse.',
  ],
  'Driver chip': [
    'This chip tells the screen what to show. It passes along the picture data to the rows and columns of tiny dots.',
    'The screen has thousands of little rows and columns. The driver chip sends each one just the right amount of electricity at just the right time, so every dot gets the right shade.',
    'A liquid crystal panel needs an exact voltage for every subpixel. Driver chips take digital picture data and convert it into precise analog voltages, sending them down the columns while a row signal selects which row is listening.',
    'Source driver chips along the edge of a panel can each handle hundreds to thousands of outputs, with 8 or 10 bit digital-to-analog converters. A 4K panel has about 8.3 million pixels and three subpixels each, redrawn around 60 times a second or more.',
  ],
  'Electron gun': [
    'The electron gun shoots a thin beam of tiny particles at the screen. Where they hit, the screen glows.',
    'At the back of the tube, a small heated piece of metal boils off electrons. Metal plates then speed them up and squeeze them into a thin beam aimed at the front glass.',
    'A heater warms a cathode until electrons escape from its surface, a process called thermionic emission. High voltage on nearby electrodes pulls the electrons forward and a focusing lens made of electric fields narrows the beam.',
    'A color tube has three guns for red, green and blue, in a single neck. The final anode voltage is about 25 thousand volts, and beam currents are tiny, only milliamps in total. The cathodes run at a few hundred volts and the heater uses about 6 volts.',
  ],
  'End cap': [
    'The end cap is the lid at the bottom. You twist it off to get to the batteries.',
    'It screws onto the tube with threads, like a jar lid. A metal spring inside it presses the batteries forward so the electricity can always connect.',
  ],
  'Flyback transformer': [
    'This part makes the very strong electricity that the picture tube needs. That strong electricity can stay inside an old TV even after it is turned off.',
    'A transformer is two coils of wire wound near each other. The flyback has a small coil in and a huge coil out, so it can turn a small push of electricity into a giant one, thousands of volts.',
    'Changing current in one coil makes a changing magnetic field, which makes a voltage in the other coil. More turns in the second coil means a higher voltage. The flyback also uses the sudden collapse of the field to make a big voltage spike.',
    'It runs at the line frequency of about 15,734 hertz, and a stack of diodes inside rectifies the pulse to make roughly 25 thousand volts for the tube anode. The tube itself acts as a capacitor that can hold charge for a long time.',
  ],
  'Focus knob': [
    'This knob makes the picture sharp. Turning it makes the glowing dots smaller and crisper.',
    'It changes the electricity going to a part of the electron gun, which squeezes the beam to a thinner point. A thinner beam makes a sharper picture.',
    'The focus electrode in the gun forms an electric lens. Changing its voltage changes how strongly the beam is squeezed, so the beam comes to a tight spot right on the screen instead of spreading out blurry.',
    'The focus voltage is a fraction of the final anode voltage, so it is several thousand volts and comes from a divider near the flyback transformer. A knob or variable resistor adjusts it.',
  ],
  'Front frame': [
    'The front frame is the border around the screen. It holds everything in place and makes the TV look neat.',
    'It is usually plastic and snaps or screws onto the back. The screen panel sits behind it, tucked into a thin edge so that you see mostly picture.',
  ],
  'Fuse': [
    'A fuse is a safety guard. If too much electricity flows, the fuse breaks on purpose and stops the flow before something gets hot.',
    'Inside is a very thin wire that is made to melt. Normal current passes through fine, but a big surge heats the thin wire until it snaps, like a tiny circuit breaker that you throw away.',
    'A fuse works because current heats a wire. The heating goes up with the square of the current, so a surge melts the thin element much faster than normal use. Once it opens, the circuit is cut. Fuses are rated by the current they pass and the voltage they can safely interrupt.',
    'Fuses are rated by current, voltage and speed. A slow blow type tolerates the short inrush surge at power on, while a fast type reacts quickly. Sand filler often helps quench the arc.',
  ],
  'Gun socket': [
    'The socket is the little cap on the very back of the tube. It plugs into the pins that feed the electron gun.',
    'The tube has thin metal pins sticking out of the back, and the socket pushes onto them like a plug. It brings in the power and signals the gun needs, including the voltage for focus.',
    'The pins connect to the heater, the cathodes, and the grids inside the gun. The socket often has a small circuit board attached, which carries the focus and screen voltages and may include spark gaps to protect the circuits from arcs.',
    'The socket carries a heater of around 6 volts, cathode drive of tens of volts of video signal swing, and focus and screen voltages in the hundreds to thousands of volts. Spark gaps on the board give a high voltage arc a harmless path instead of damaging the video circuits.',
  ],
  'HDMI cable': [
    'This cable carries the picture and sound from a box, like a game console, into the TV.',
    'Inside are many tiny wires. Some carry the picture, some the sound, and some let the two devices talk to each other and agree on what they can show.',
    'HDMI sends digital data. Picture data travels on four pairs of wires, using signals called differential pairs, where the information is the difference in voltage between the two wires. This helps cancel noise picked up along the way.',
    'HDMI 2.0 carries up to 18 billion bits per second, and 2.1 up to 48 billion. Data is encoded so the signal stays balanced, and a separate channel lets the TV tell the source what resolutions it supports.',
  ],
  'HDMI port': [
    'This is the socket where a game console or other box plugs in. The picture and sound come in through here.',
    'The port has small metal contacts in a neat row that line up with the plug. A shield around it blocks stray noise, and the chips behind it read the incoming data.',
    'The incoming signal is a fast digital stream. A chip behind the port, called a receiver, turns it back into picture and sound data. HDMI also supports a copy protection system called HDCP.',
    'An HDMI connector has 19 pins. The receiver recovers a clock from the data and decodes the 8 or 10 bit color values. A small memory in the TV reports its supported modes to the source through a pin called display data channel.',
  ],
  'Heat sink': [
    'A heat sink is like a radiator. Some parts get warm, and the heat sink pulls the heat away so they stay safe.',
    'It is a block of metal with lots of fins. Metal carries heat well, and the fins give the heat a big surface to escape into the air around it.',
    'Heat flows from hot to cool by conduction through the metal, then moves into the air by convection and a little radiation. More fin area means more heat leaves each second. Aluminum is popular because it is light and conducts heat well.',
    'Designers think in thermal resistance, in degrees per watt. A part that wastes 10 watts on a heat sink with 5 degrees per watt rises about 50 degrees above the air around it. Thermal paste or pads fill tiny air gaps between the part and the metal.',
  ],
  'High-voltage wire': [
    'This thick wire carries the strongest electricity in the TV, to the picture tube. Its heavy rubber coat keeps it safe.',
    'It has a thick insulating coat, much thicker than normal wires, because electricity at such a high voltage tries hard to jump out. The wire ends at a rubber cup stuck onto the tube.',
    'Voltage is the push on electricity, and a very high push can jump through air or thin insulation as a spark. Thick insulation and a rounded cup keep the field from concentrating and leaking away as a corona or arc.',
    'It carries roughly 25 thousand volts to the anode button on the side of the tube, but only a tiny current. Insulation is silicone or similar rubber, rated for well above the working voltage. The glass funnel is coated inside and out to act as a capacitor.',
  ],
  'LCD panel': [
    'This is the screen itself. It makes the picture by letting just the right amount of light through each tiny dot.',
    'The panel is a sandwich. A layer of liquid crystal sits between sheets of glass and filters, and a tiny switch for every dot decides how much light gets through. Red, green and blue dots mix into every color.',
    'Liquid crystals are molecules that can twist. Light first passes through a polarizer, which only lets waves that vibrate in one direction through. Voltage changes the twist, and a second polarizer then blocks more or less of the light.',
    'A thin film transistor at every subpixel sets an electric field across the liquid crystal, so there are about 25 million of them in a 4K panel. A color filter gives each subpixel its red, green or blue, and only a small fraction of the backlight finally exits the front.',
  ],
  'LED bulb': [
    'This is the part that makes the light. It uses a tiny chip instead of a hot, glowing wire, so it wastes less heat and lasts a long time.',
    'A small chip sits inside a clear dome with two metal legs. When electricity goes through, the chip glows. The dome helps the light spread out in front instead of every direction.',
    'LED means light emitting diode. Electrons crossing the diode junction fall into lower energy states and release the extra energy as photons, which are tiny packets of light.',
    'A white LED is usually a blue chip with a yellow phosphor coating, and the mix looks white. Efficiency is far higher than an old filament bulb, which turns most of its power into heat, and a good LED can last tens of thousands of hours.',
  ],
  'LED cap': [
    'This clear little dome covers each light and helps shape where the light goes.',
    'The cap is clear plastic or silicone molded into a tiny lens. It protects the delicate chip underneath and sends the light out in the direction you want.',
    'A dome lens bends light as it passes from the material into air, which is called refraction. The shape can spread the light wide, so each tiny LED covers more of the screen behind it.',
    'Encapsulants are usually silicone or epoxy chosen to stay clear under heat and bright blue light. Their refractive index helps get more light out of the chip, because light inside a high index chip is easily trapped by total internal reflection.',
  ],
  'LED chip': [
    'The chip is the tiny glowing heart of the light. It is smaller than a grain of rice.',
    'It is a small piece of special crystal with thin wires attached. When electricity flows through, the crystal glows with one color. A blue chip with a yellow coating can make white.',
    'The chip is a semiconductor with a p-n junction. The material sets the energy gap, and the gap sets the color of the photons. Blue LEDs use gallium nitride, which is why they were hard to invent and won a Nobel Prize in 2014.',
    'A small LED chip may be about 1 millimeter across and run on 3 volts with tens of milliamps of current. A thin layer of quantum wells inside traps electrons and holes together so they combine and release light efficiently, and the rest of the energy leaves as heat.',
  ],
  'LED driver chip': [
    'This chip is the dimmer for the backlight. It controls how bright the little lights are.',
    'Many LEDs need a steady, careful amount of electricity, not too much and not too little. This chip makes sure each string of lights gets just that, and can dim them quickly.',
    'LED brightness is controlled by current. The chip regulates the current through each string, and can dim by switching it on and off very fast, a method called pulse width modulation, so the eye sees an average brightness.',
    'Backlight strings can need tens of volts, so the driver often includes a boost converter. Dimming happens at hundreds or thousands of cycles per second to avoid visible flicker. With local dimming, separate zones are dimmed to improve contrast.',
  ],
  'LED light': [
    'Each little light here helps light up the screen from behind. Together they make the glow you see.',
    'The TV has many small LEDs lined up in rows. Each one is bright and uses very little power, and together they light the whole screen evenly.',
    'LEDs are semiconductors that turn current directly into light. Because each one is tiny and efficient, a TV can use dozens or hundreds of them without making as much heat. Many TVs wire them in strings so they share the same current.',
    'In a direct lit design, LEDs sit behind the panel in rows and dim in zones, while in an edge lit design they sit along the sides. Strings are driven at a constant current, because the voltage across an LED varies with temperature.',
  ],
  'LED strip': [
    'A row of little lights on one strip. Lots of strips side by side light up the whole screen.',
    'The strip is a long, thin circuit board with the tiny lights soldered on in a line. The board also carries the copper paths that bring electricity to each one.',
    'The LEDs on a strip are wired in series, one after another, so that the same current runs through each. That keeps their brightness the same. A white or silver coating on the board reflects light that would otherwise be lost.',
    'Strips are often aluminum core boards, which move heat away from the LEDs better than normal fiberglass boards. Keeping an LED cool matters because brightness and lifespan both drop as the junction gets hot.',
  ],
  'Lanyard loop': [
    'This little loop is where you can tie a string, so the flashlight is easy to carry and hard to lose.',
    'It is a small ring or hole on the end of the case. You thread a cord through it and loop it around your wrist or hang it from a hook.',
  ],
  'Lens': [
    'The clear lens at the front protects the light and helps shape the beam.',
    'It is a clear piece of plastic or glass that seals the front, keeping dust and water out. Some lenses are curved, which bends the light so it goes where you aim.',
    'A lens bends light by refraction, because light moves slower in glass than in air. A curved lens can make rays more parallel, creating a narrow beam, or spread them out into a wide, soft one.',
    'Flashlight lenses are often polycarbonate or acrylic, and some have textured surfaces to smooth the beam. Many designs pair a lens with a reflector, and focus works by moving the light source relative to them.',
  ],
  'Light messenger chip': [
    'This chip sends a message with light, so that two parts of the TV can talk without touching.',
    'Inside are a tiny LED and a light sensor, side by side with a gap between them. The first part blinks, the second sees it, and the message gets across without any wire joining them.',
    'This part is called an optocoupler. It carries a signal across a gap using light, so the two sides share no electrical connection. That keeps the dangerous wall power side separate from the safe low voltage side.',
    'Optocouplers are rated for isolation of several thousand volts. In a power supply, one sits in the feedback path so the control chip on the wall side can learn the output voltage from the safe side. Their gain drifts with age, which designers must allow for.',
  ],
  'Magnet': [
    'A magnet pulls on some metals and can push or pull on other magnets. Here, the magnet helps make sound.',
    'The speaker has a strong magnet sitting behind a coil of wire. When electricity flows in the coil, it becomes a magnet too, and the two push and pull on each other, making the cone move.',
    'A magnet has a magnetic field around it. A wire carrying current in that field feels a force, called the Lorentz force. This is the effect that makes speakers and electric motors work.',
    'Speaker magnets are usually ferrite, a ceramic, or neodymium in small, slim designs. The field is strongest in the narrow air gap where the coil sits. A stronger gap field means more force for the same current.',
  ],
  'Main board': [
    'This is the TV brain. It takes in the picture and sound and decides what the screen and speakers should do.',
    'A flat green board with lots of chips and tiny copper paths. The paths carry signals between the chips, a bit like the roads between buildings in a town.',
    'A printed circuit board is layers of copper traces separated by fiberglass insulation, with chips and parts soldered on. The main board holds the processor, memory, tuner and ports, and runs the software that makes the TV smart.',
    'Main boards typically have four to eight layers, with controlled impedance traces for fast signals such as memory and video links. The main chip is a system on chip that combines processor cores, video decoder and graphics, running on a core voltage near 1 volt.',
  ],
  'Main circuit board': [
    'This is the TV brain. It takes in the picture signal and the sound signal and controls everything else.',
    'It is a flat board with lots of little parts joined by metal lines. Signals come in from the antenna and the knobs, and the board sends the right signals out to the tube and the speaker.',
    'In an older TV the board holds the tuner, the circuits that pull out picture and sound, and the amplifiers that boost them. Transistors and other parts do the work, and the board connects them to each other.',
    'A tube TV chassis mixes analog circuits. A tuner picks the channel, an intermediate frequency stage at around 45 megahertz amplifies it, a detector pulls out video and audio, and amplifiers drive the tube at tens of volts and the speaker at a few watts.',
  ],
  'Memory chip': [
    'The memory chip is the TV short-term notebook. It holds the thing the TV is working on right now.',
    'When the TV shows a picture or runs an app, it needs room to keep the information close at hand. This chip provides that room, and everything in it is forgotten when the TV turns off.',
    'This is RAM, which stands for random access memory. Each bit is stored as a tiny charge in a capacitor, and the chip must refresh it many times a second or it fades. It is fast to read and write.',
    'A smart TV commonly has 2 to 4 gigabytes of DRAM, such as DDR4. Memory runs at billions of transfers per second on a bus tuned for speed. Video frames, graphics and app data all live there while the TV is on.',
  ],
  'Network port': [
    'This socket lets you plug in an internet cable, so the TV can get shows from the internet.',
    'A network cable has small wires twisted in pairs. The port passes the information on to a chip, which handles the internet talk for the TV.',
    'It is an Ethernet port. The data travels as electrical pulses on twisted pairs of wire, and the twisting helps cancel out noise. A small transformer inside the port keeps the TV electrically separate from the cable.',
    'Typical TVs support 100 megabits or 1 gigabit per second. The connector is called an 8P8C, often called an RJ45, and the isolation transformer is rated for about 1500 volts. Many TVs rely on Wi-Fi, so the port may go unused.',
  ],
  'Picture cable plug': [
    'This is the plug at the end of the picture cable. It snaps into a socket so the picture can travel through.',
    'The plug has many tiny metal contacts in a tight row. Each one lines up with a contact in the socket, so each thin wire in the cable has its own path.',
    'Many separate signals have to arrive at the right place, so the contacts have to be lined up accurately. A latch or clip keeps the plug from wiggling loose, because a bad connection can give flickering or wrong colors.',
    'These connectors use fine pitch contacts, often around half a millimeter apart, and a locking mechanism. Contacts are gold plated or tin plated, and the signals running through them are fast differential pairs.',
  ],
  'Picture cable socket': [
    'This is the socket that the picture cable plugs into. It holds the cable tight so the picture can travel through.',
    'The socket has little metal fingers that press against the plug contacts. A small clip locks the plug in place so it cannot shake loose.',
    'Each little spring contact squeezes the plug, which makes a reliable electrical connection and a little scraping action that cleans the metal. The socket is soldered to the circuit board by its pins.',
    'Board to cable sockets of this type are often zero insertion force, where a flip latch clamps the flat cable after it is inserted. That avoids damage to the delicate contacts and gives consistent contact force over many years.',
  ],
  'Picture cable': [
    'This cable carries the picture from the main board to the screen, like a little road for the picture.',
    'It has many thin wires side by side. The picture is sent as a stream of numbers, and different wires carry different parts of it so the whole picture can get across fast.',
    'The picture travels as digital data on pairs of wires using low voltage differential signaling. The difference in voltage between the two wires of each pair carries the information, which makes it tough for noise to garble it.',
    'LVDS pairs swing about 350 millivolts around a level near 1.2 volts. A panel might use several pairs, each carrying hundreds of millions of bits per second, plus a clock pair. Cables are kept short and shielded to keep the signals clean.',
  ],
  'Pocket clip': [
    'The clip lets you hang the flashlight on a pocket or a belt so your hands stay free.',
    'It is a strip of bendy metal that acts like a spring. You slide the edge of a pocket under it, and the spring pushes the cloth against the body of the flashlight and holds on.',
  ],
  'Power board': [
    'This board takes wall power and turns it into the right kind of electricity for each part of the TV.',
    'The wall gives strong power that changes direction. The TV parts want gentle power that flows one way. The power board changes the one into the other and keeps it steady.',
    'It is a switching power supply. It rectifies wall power, chops it up very fast with a transistor, passes it through a small transformer and rectifies it again, producing several lower, steady voltages.',
    'Switching at tens to hundreds of thousands of cycles per second lets the transformer be small and about 85 to 90 percent efficient. Feedback through an optocoupler adjusts the chopping to hold the output steady. Many designs include power factor correction.',
  ],
  'Power button': [
    'Press this to turn the TV on or off.',
    'The button presses on a small switch on a board. When the TV is off it is not completely asleep, a tiny part stays awake just to notice you pressing it.',
  ],
  'Power chip': [
    'This chip is a careful power helper. It makes sure each part of the main board gets exactly the voltage it needs.',
    'Chips are picky. One needs a little power and another needs a bit more. This chip takes in one voltage and gives out smooth, exact ones for the others.',
    'A voltage regulator holds its output steady even when the input or the load changes. A linear regulator wastes extra voltage as heat, while a switching one chops the power and is more efficient.',
    'Processors in a TV run on core voltages near 1 volt and draw several amps in bursts. Regulators must respond within microseconds, and are placed near the chips with capacitors close by to supply sudden current demands.',
  ],
  'Power control chip': [
    'This chip is the manager of the power board. It tells the big switch when to turn on and off.',
    'It watches how much electricity comes out and adjusts. If the TV needs more power, it keeps the switch on longer, and if it needs less, it keeps it off longer.',
    'This is a pulse width modulation controller. It makes a train of pulses and changes the width of each one, based on feedback about the output voltage. A wider pulse gives more energy and a narrower one gives less.',
    'Controllers often include protection against overcurrent, overvoltage and overheating, and a burst mode for standby that saves energy, using less than half a watt. The switching rate is commonly between 65 and 130 thousand cycles per second.',
  ],
  'Power cord': [
    'The cord brings electricity from the wall into the machine. Never pull on it with wet hands, and always ask a grown-up to unplug.',
    'Inside the plastic coat are copper wires that carry the electricity. A third pin on some plugs is a safety wire to carry stray electricity away to the ground.',
    'The copper is stranded to bend without breaking, and thick enough to carry the current without getting hot. The outer insulation keeps the electricity inside. A ground wire gives faults a safe path if a live wire touches the metal case.',
    'Cord thickness is picked by amperage, so a TV cord is often 18 gauge, rated for about 10 amps. In the United States, the wall supplies about 120 volts at 60 hertz. Polarized plugs make the neutral and live wires line up the same way every time.',
  ],
  'Power socket': [
    'This is the hole where the power cord plugs in at the back of the TV.',
    'Inside the socket are metal contacts that grip the plug pins tightly. This gives a solid connection so the electricity flows easily and the cord does not wiggle loose.',
    'The contacts are springy metal that squeeze each pin, keeping the connection good with a lot of surface touching. Bad contact makes heat, so they need to be tight and made from good conducting metal like brass.',
    'Inlet sockets are rated for a given current and voltage, and often include the fuse holder or an electrical noise filter. Some have a second pin for ground, and the plastic body must resist heat and flames.',
  ],
  'Power switch': [
    'This is a tiny, super fast switch that turns the power on and off over and over. It helps make the right amount of electricity.',
    'It has no moving parts. A small signal on one pin lets a big current flow between the other two, so it works like a faucet that you can open and close very quickly.',
    'This is a MOSFET, a kind of transistor. A voltage on the gate makes an electric field that opens a conducting channel between the source and drain. It can switch on and off hundreds of thousands of times a second.',
    'Power MOSFETs for this job are often rated for about 600 volts and have an on resistance of a fraction of an ohm. Most of the heat comes from the moments of switching and from the small resistance while on, which is why it sits on a heat sink.',
  ],
  'Power transistor': [
    'This is a strong electric switch. It uses a small signal to control a lot of electricity.',
    'A transistor has three legs. A tiny current into one leg lets a much bigger current flow between the other two, like a small hand turning a big tap.',
    'A transistor is a semiconductor device that can switch or amplify. In a bipolar transistor, a small current at the base controls a much larger current from collector to emitter. This is how weak signals become strong ones.',
    'The one that drives the flyback transformer must handle high voltage pulses of over a thousand volts, at a current of several amps. It dissipates a fair amount of heat, so it is bolted to a heat sink, and a damper diode may sit with it.',
  ],
  'Power wire socket': [
    'This socket is where the wires from the power board plug in. It carries electricity to the parts that need it.',
    'The plug and socket join several wires at once, and the shape only lets them fit one way. That stops anyone from connecting them backward.',
    'Connectors like this use metal contacts crimped onto each wire. The plastic housing is keyed, so it only fits the right way, which prevents wrong connections that could harm the circuit.',
    'The contacts are rated for a set current, often one to a few amps per pin. Voltage levels like 12 or 24 volts are common here, and more than one pin is often used in parallel to carry more current.',
  ],
  'Power wires': [
    'These wires carry electricity from the power board to the other parts. They are like little pipes for power.',
    'Each wire is copper inside a colored plastic coat. Wires are bundled together and plugged into the boards, so the power board can send electricity wherever it is needed.',
    'Copper is used because it conducts electricity well. The plastic coat insulates the metal, and different colors help the person assembling it know what each wire is for. Wire thickness is chosen for how much current it carries.',
    'Thicker wire has lower resistance and so loses less voltage and gets less warm. A harness like this is made from stranded copper, often 22 to 18 gauge, with insulation rated for 300 volts or more.',
  ],
  'Processor chip': [
    'This chip is the thinker. It follows instructions to run the menus, apps and picture.',
    'Inside the chip are billions of tiny switches called transistors. They flip on and off very fast, following a list of instructions, to do math and make decisions.',
    'A processor fetches an instruction, decodes it, and runs it, again and again at billions of times a second. Each instruction is a simple operation on numbers. All the software, from menus to video apps, is built from them.',
    'TV chips are systems on chip, packing several processor cores, a video decoder, a graphics unit and often a neural network engine on one piece of silicon. They are built with a manufacturing process measured in nanometers and run at about 1 to 2 gigahertz.',
  ],
  'Reflector sheet': [
    'This white sheet bounces light forward, so none of the light is wasted heading backward.',
    'It sits behind the lights like a mirror made of white plastic. Light that goes the wrong way hits it and bounces back toward the screen.',
    'The sheet is white because it scatters light in all directions instead of absorbing it. That is called diffuse reflection. Reflecting the light forward means a brighter picture without using more electricity.',
    'Reflective films are made of plastic with tiny bubbles or white pigment, and can reflect over 95 percent of visible light. That cuts the number of LEDs needed, and so the heat and power consumption.',
  ],
  'Reflector': [
    'The reflector is a shiny cup around the light. It catches light going sideways and sends it forward in a beam.',
    'It is shaped like a bowl with a mirror finish. Light from the bulb hits the sides and bounces toward the front, so more of it goes where you point.',
    'A curved mirror can control the direction of light. A parabola shape turns light from a source at its center into a nearly parallel beam. Reflection follows a simple rule, where the angle in equals the angle out.',
    'Reflectors are often metalized plastic or aluminum, with a smooth finish for a tight beam or a slightly textured one for a smooth spot. The beam shape depends on the reflector shape and on exactly where the LED sits in it.',
  ],
  'Remote sensor': [
    'This little window watches for the invisible light from your remote. When you press a button, it sees the flash and tells the TV.',
    'The remote has a small light that blinks out a secret pattern of flashes you cannot see. The sensor spots the flashes and turns them into a message, like volume up or channel down.',
    'The remote sends infrared light, which is just past red in the spectrum, and your eyes cannot see it. A photodiode in the sensor turns the light pulses into small electrical pulses, which a chip decodes into a button code.',
    'Infrared remotes usually modulate the light at about 38 thousand cycles per second, so the sensor can ignore steady background light such as the sun or lamps. The wavelength is around 940 nanometers, and the range is a few meters.',
  ],
  'Resistor': [
    'A resistor holds back the flow of electricity, a bit like a narrow spot in a pipe. It helps keep the flow just right.',
    'Inside is a material that does not let electricity through easily, so some of the push is used up. Colored stripes on the outside are a code that tells you how strong the resistor is.',
    'Resistance is how hard it is for current to flow, measured in ohms. Ohm law says voltage equals current times resistance. A resistor can limit current, divide a voltage, or set the timing of a circuit.',
    'Common resistors are carbon film or metal film, with tolerances of 1 to 5 percent. They turn the electrical energy they resist into heat, so they have a power rating, often a quarter watt. Stripes encode the value and tolerance.',
  ],
  'Ribbon cable': [
    'A flat cable with lots of thin wires side by side. Each wire carries its own little signal.',
    'Many wires sit next to each other in one flat strip, so they stay neat and bend easily. This helps fit lots of connections into a thin TV.',
    'A flexible flat cable has thin copper strips laminated between plastic layers. Each strip is a separate wire, and the strips carry signals in parallel, so a lot of information can travel at once.',
    'Flat flexible cables use a pitch from 0.3 to 1 millimeter, and thickness of a fraction of a millimeter. They can bend many times without breaking, though a sharp crease can crack the copper, and they are often shielded for high speed signals.',
  ],
  'Rubber ring': [
    'This little rubber ring squishes tight to keep things sealed and snug.',
    'Rubber is squishy but springs back. The ring is pressed between two parts, filling tiny gaps, so water and dust cannot sneak through.',
  ],
  'Safety capacitor': [
    'This is a special capacitor that helps keep you safe. It helps keep electrical noise from leaking out of the TV.',
    'It sits between the dangerous wall side and the safe low voltage side. It is built extra strong, so even if it wears out, it fails in a safe way and does not cause a shock.',
    'Electronics make high frequency noise. This capacitor gives noise a path to go away through, but it is made so that only a very tiny, safe current can pass at wall frequency. Safety rules require special construction and testing.',
    'These are often called Y capacitors, with values of just a few nanofarads, typically 1 to 4.7. A Y class part is rated to withstand a voltage of thousands of volts, and must fail open rather than short. It is the reason some TV frames tingle faintly.',
  ],
  'Screw': [
    'A screw is a metal bolt that holds things together. You turn it with a screwdriver.',
    'The spiral ridge on a screw is called a thread. When you turn it, the thread bites in and pulls the parts closer, like a tiny ramp wrapped round and round.',
  ],
  'Settings memory': [
    'This tiny memory remembers your settings when the TV is off, like the volume and your favorite picture look.',
    'Most memory forgets as soon as the power goes off. This kind does not. It stores a small amount of information that stays safe, even with the TV unplugged.',
    'It is a kind of non-volatile memory called an EEPROM. Information is stored as trapped electric charge in tiny transistors, which holds for many years without power. It can be erased and rewritten electronically.',
    'Typical sizes are a few kilobits to a few hundred kilobits. It talks to the main chip over a simple two wire bus, and is rated for about a million write cycles and data retention of around 40 years.',
  ],
  'Side-to-side coil': [
    'This coil pushes the beam left and right across the screen, line after line.',
    'The coil is a magnet that you can control with electricity. When the coil is on, it pulls the beam to one side, and as the electricity changes it moves the beam across the screen.',
    'A magnetic field exerts a force on moving electrons. Current through this coil changes the field strength, so the beam moves smoothly sideways. This is how the beam draws each line of the picture.',
    'The horizontal deflection runs at about 15,734 lines per second, with a sawtooth current of a few amps. The fast flyback of the beam to the start of the next line is when the flyback transformer gets its name.',
  ],
  'Sound chip': [
    'This chip makes the sound. It takes the sound signal and makes it strong enough to run the speaker.',
    'The sound signal starts out very weak. The chip makes it bigger, like a megaphone for electricity, so it has enough strength to move the speaker.',
    'The chip is an audio amplifier. It takes a small varying voltage that copies the shape of a sound wave and produces a copy of it with much more power. Some also decode digital sound into an analog signal.',
    'Small TV amplifiers deliver around 5 to 10 watts per channel, often Class D designs that switch at hundreds of thousands of times a second for high efficiency. Older tube TVs used a linear Class AB amplifier of a watt or two.',
  ],
  'Speaker cone': [
    'The cone is the part that moves back and forth to make sound. Moving air makes the sound you hear.',
    'The cone is a stiff but light paper or plastic bowl. It is attached to a coil, and when the coil moves, the whole cone pushes the air in front of it.',
    'Sound is a wave of air pressure. The cone pushes the air forward and pulls it back, making pressure waves that travel to your ear. Faster back and forth wiggling gives a higher pitch, and bigger wiggles give a louder sound.',
    'Cones are made from paper, polypropylene or similar materials that are light yet stiff. A flexible edge called the surround and a spider hold it centered. Small TV speakers run best for midrange and voice, with limited deep bass.',
  ],
  'Small capacitor': [
    'A small capacitor is a tiny bucket for electricity. It catches little bumps and wobbles so the chips get calm, steady power.',
    'Chips switch on and off very fast and ask for little gulps of power. A small capacitor sits right beside them and hands over each gulp instantly, so the power does not wobble.',
    'A capacitor stores charge on two plates with an insulator between them. A small one holds little charge but can give it up very quickly, which lets it smooth fast changes that a big capacitor is too slow to catch.',
    'Many small capacitors on boards are ceramic, with values from picofarads to a few microfarads. Placed close to a chip, they act as local decoupling, giving a path for fast current spikes and keeping noise off the power lines.',
  ],
  'Speaker plug': [
    'This plug connects the speaker to the board, so the sound signal can get to it.',
    'It is a small plastic piece that has two metal contacts, one for each speaker wire. It pushes onto the board pins and can only go on one way.',
  ],
  'Speaker wire': [
    'Thin wires carry the sound signal from the board to the speaker.',
    'There are two wires, because electricity needs a full loop to flow. The signal goes out on one wire and comes back on the other, changing direction many times each second.',
    'The sound signal is alternating current. The copper wire is thin because speakers need only a little current, but too thin or too long a wire would waste some of the signal as resistance.',
    'For a short run to an eight ohm speaker, a thin wire like 24 gauge adds only a small fraction of an ohm. The wires are usually stranded copper so that they can flex without breaking.',
  ],
  'Speaker': [
    'The speaker turns electricity into sound. It shakes the air so that you can hear your show.',
    'A speaker has a magnet, a coil of wire and a cone. The electric signal makes the coil move back and forth in the magnet, and that shakes the cone and the air.',
    'A speaker turns an electrical signal into sound. The coil in a magnetic field feels a force that follows the signal, so the cone moves in the same pattern, and the moving air carries the sound to you.',
    'A typical TV speaker has an impedance of 8 ohms and handles a few watts. A small cone is weak at low bass, so thin TVs rely on processing to sound fuller.',
  ],
  'Spring': [
    'The spring pushes the batteries forward so they always touch, even when you bump the flashlight.',
    'It is a coil of metal that springs back when you squeeze it. It presses on one end of the batteries, keeping the metal contacts tightly touching, so the electricity can flow.',
    'A spring stores energy when compressed and pushes back in proportion to how far it is squeezed. Here it also carries electricity, since it is metal, completing the circuit from the battery to the case.',
    'Contact springs are often steel with nickel or gold plating to resist rust. They must provide enough force to keep a low resistance contact in the face of vibration, but not so much that it makes the cap hard to turn.',
  ],
  'Stand': [
    'The stand holds the TV up so it does not fall over.',
    'It is wide at the bottom to spread the weight out. A wide base makes the TV harder to tip over, and it also holds the screen at a good height to watch.',
  ],
  'Standby light': [
    'This little light shows the TV is asleep but plugged in, ready to wake when you press the remote.',
    'Even when the screen is dark, a small part of the TV stays awake. The light shows that this part is working and waiting for you to press a button.',
    'Standby mode keeps a small circuit powered so the TV can listen for the remote. An indicator LED shows the state. Modern standby uses very little power, as the main parts are switched off.',
    'Regulations push standby draw below about half a watt. The indicator LED itself takes only a few milliamps through a resistor, and the remote sensor and a small microcontroller stay powered from a low power supply.',
  ],
  'Surge guard': [
    'This part protects the TV from sudden big jolts of electricity, like those that can come in during a storm.',
    'It is a small disc that usually acts like an open door. When a big jolt comes, the disc suddenly lets electricity pass through to the side, and the jolt goes away from the TV.',
    'This is a varistor, which stands for variable resistor. Its resistance is very high at normal voltage and drops sharply above a threshold, so a voltage spike is shorted out and absorbed rather than reaching the delicate parts.',
    'It is usually a metal oxide varistor, made of zinc oxide grains. A 120 volt line would use a part rated a bit above the normal line voltage. Each absorbed surge slowly wears it out, and it is often paired with a fuse in case it fails.',
  ],
  'Switch contact': [
    'This little piece of metal touches another piece of metal and lets the electricity pass. When you press the switch, they connect.',
    'The switch has two pieces of metal that touch or move apart. When they touch, the circuit is complete and electricity flows. When they are apart, there is a gap and nothing flows.',
    'A switch works because electricity needs a closed loop. Pressing the switch brings the conductors together and closes the loop, and the current flows. Contacts are made of springy metal, so they press firmly together.',
    'Contacts are often brass or copper with plating that resists oxidation. A tiny arc can form when the contacts open, which slowly wears them down. Low current flashlight switches can last for tens of thousands of presses.',
  ],
  'Switch': [
    'The switch turns the flashlight on and off. It opens and closes the path for electricity.',
    'Inside, two metal pieces move together or apart. When they touch, the path for the electricity is complete and the light goes on. When they separate, the path is broken and it goes out.',
    'A circuit works only when it forms a complete loop from the battery, through the light and back. A switch breaks or completes that loop. A spring inside lets a button click and return to its position.',
    'Common flashlight switches are push button, with a click mechanism, or twist type. The contacts need a low resistance, because even a fraction of an ohm wastes some of the battery power as heat, especially at higher currents.',
  ],
  'Storage chip': [
    'This chip is the TV long-term memory. It keeps the TV software and apps even when the power is off.',
    'Unlike the short-term memory, this chip does not forget when the TV is unplugged. The TV reads its instructions from here every time it wakes up.',
    'This is flash memory. Bits are stored as electric charge trapped on a tiny floating gate inside each cell, and the charge stays put with no power. Erasing and rewriting is possible, but it slowly wears the cells out.',
    'TV storage is often 8 to 16 gigabytes of NAND flash, holding the operating system, apps and settings. Cells survive thousands of erase cycles, so controllers spread writes evenly and set aside spare blocks for ones that wear out.',
  ],
  'T-con board': [
    'This small board is the timing boss for the screen. It tells every part of the screen exactly when to change.',
    'The picture has millions of dots, and every one has to change at the right moment, like a huge group of dancers. The timing board keeps everyone in step.',
    'T-con is short for timing controller. It takes the picture data from the main board and re-times it into the exact stream of rows and columns the panel needs, and creates the clock signals for the drivers.',
    'A T-con handles data at many gigabits per second, and often does extras such as overdrive, which speeds up pixel changes, and gamma adjustment. The panel refreshes at 60 to 120 times per second, so every frame is drawn in roughly 8 to 17 milliseconds.',
  ],
  'TV tuner': [
    'The tuner picks one channel out of the many signals the antenna catches, like turning the radio dial to one station.',
    'Lots of channels arrive at the same time. The tuner has a part that is tuned to just one of them, and it ignores all the rest, so you only get the show you chose.',
    'Each channel uses a different band of frequencies. The tuner mixes the incoming signal with a signal it makes itself, which shifts the chosen channel to a fixed frequency that the rest of the circuits can easily handle.',
    'This is a superheterodyne design. A local oscillator is set by the channel you pick, and mixing creates the difference frequency, often an intermediate frequency of about 36 to 44 megahertz, which is filtered precisely. Digital TV then decodes the data.',
  ],
  'Timing chip': [
    'This chip keeps the screen in time. It tells the screen when to start each new line of the picture.',
    'The screen is drawn line by line, again and again. The timing chip makes sure each line starts at exactly the right moment, so the picture stays in place and does not shake.',
    'Digital video comes with timing signals that say when each line and each frame begins. The timing controller chip uses them to produce the exact clocks and control pulses that the panel drivers need.',
    'It generates signals tied to the pixel clock, which for a 4K picture at 60 frames per second is about 600 million cycles per second. Precise timing with a variation of tiny fractions of a nanosecond is needed for a clean image.',
  ],
  'Transformer': [
    'A transformer changes the strength of electricity. It can take a big push and make it gentle, or the other way around.',
    'It has two coils of wire wound around the same core, but not touching. When electricity flows in one coil, it makes a magnet field that sends electricity into the other coil.',
    'A changing current in the first coil makes a changing magnetic field in the core, which makes a voltage in the second coil. The voltage ratio equals the ratio of the number of turns. It only works with changing current.',
    'A switching power supply transformer runs at tens of thousands of cycles per second on a ferrite core, so it can be small. It also provides electrical isolation between the wall side and the output, typically rated for thousands of volts.',
  ],
  'Tuner': [
    'The tuner picks a channel out of all the signals coming from the antenna, like turning a radio dial.',
    'Many stations are in the air at once. The tuner is tuned to one and ignores the others. When you turn the channel knob, you change which one it listens to.',
    'Each station sends on its own frequency. The tuner uses a circuit that resonates at just one frequency, so it strongly picks up that one and mostly rejects the rest. The chosen signal is then amplified.',
    'Old tuners used a coil and a variable capacitor or a switched set of coils. VHF channels range from about 54 to 216 megahertz in the US, and the UHF band is higher. The tuner shifts the chosen channel to a fixed intermediate frequency for processing.',
  ],
  'USB port': [
    'The USB port lets you plug in things like a memory stick to watch pictures or videos.',
    'Inside the port are four little metal contacts. Two carry power so the device can run, and two carry the information back and forth.',
    'USB sends data as a stream of high and low voltages on a pair of wires, and supplies 5 volts of power on another pair. The TV chip checks for a device being plugged in and asks it what it is.',
    'USB 2.0 runs at up to 480 million bits per second and USB 3 at 5 billion or more. A TV port typically supplies 5 volts at 0.5 to 1 amp, enough for a memory stick or small drive, and signals use differential pairs for noise resistance.',
  ],
  'Up-and-down coil': [
    'This coil pushes the beam up and down, so it can move from the top of the screen to the bottom.',
    'Like its side-to-side partner, this coil is a controllable magnet. It slowly moves the beam down the screen, and then quickly snaps it back to the top to start over.',
    'The magnetic field from this coil pushes the electron beam vertically. A current that slowly rises and then quickly resets moves the beam from top to bottom and then back, drawing the full picture.',
    'Vertical deflection runs at about 60 times per second, much slower than the horizontal one. In an interlaced picture, it draws all the odd lines first, then the even lines, making about 30 full pictures every second.',
  ],
  'Volume knob': [
    'Turn this knob to make the sound louder or quieter.',
    'The knob turns a part that changes how much of the sound signal passes through. Turn it one way and more gets through, so the sound is louder.',
    'It turns a variable resistor, also called a potentiometer. Turning the knob slides a contact along a strip of resistive material, so it changes how much of the voltage is passed on to the amplifier.',
    'Volume controls use a logarithmic taper, because hearing responds to loudness on a logarithmic scale. A tenfold increase in power sounds only about twice as loud, so the control must change the level more gently at first.',
  ],
  'Wi-Fi module': [
    'This little part lets the TV talk to your home internet with radio waves, without a cable.',
    'It has a tiny radio inside that sends and listens for signals from your router, the box that connects your home to the internet. Information travels through the air as radio waves.',
    'Wi-Fi sends digital data using radio waves, typically in the 2.4 and 5 gigahertz bands. The module turns bits into radio signals and back, and a small antenna on the board sends and catches the waves.',
    'A module combines a radio, a baseband chip and a small antenna, often using two streams for speeds of hundreds of megabits per second. Many different devices share the same bands, so it takes turns and listens before it talks.',
  ],
  'Wooden leg': [
    'The wooden legs hold the old TV up off the floor, like the legs of a table.',
    'In old TV sets, the cabinet was often made of real wood. The legs are strong enough to hold the heavy picture tube, which is made of thick glass.',
  ],
  'Yellow coating': [
    'This yellow layer changes the color of the light. It helps turn the blue light into white light.',
    'The little chip glows blue, but your TV needs white light. A thin yellow powder on top soaks up some blue light and glows yellow. Blue and yellow together look white.',
    'The coating is called a phosphor. It absorbs high energy blue photons and re-emits lower energy yellow photons. Our eyes mix the leftover blue and the new yellow and see white light.',
    'A common phosphor is cerium doped yttrium aluminum garnet, mixed in silicone and applied over a blue chip. Some energy is lost as heat in the conversion. Mixing the ratio sets how warm or cool the white looks.',
  ],
};
